  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis early this afternoon depict \nnorthwest flow aloft over the central/northern Plains in the wake of \nan upper wave traversing the Great Lakes. Meanwhile, a mid/upper \nridge builds over portions of the Northern Rockies and Central Great \nBasin. At the surface, a cold front extends from the Great Lakes \ninto the southern Plains. Despite relatively drier air behind this \nboundary, thermal profiles from short-term guidance indicate that \nradiational cooling later tonight may allow for patches of ground \nfog especially across areas north of US-50 heading into Monday \nmorning. Dense fog is not expected at this time.\n\nComfortable conditions are anticipated areawide to start the work \nweek, with seasonable high temperatures in the mid 70s forecast for \nMonday afternoon as winds come back around to the south. For \nTuesday, a midlevel impulse tracking east across the Canadian \nPrairie will shunt a weak surface trough into the Plains. This will \nserve to tighten the surface pressure gradient over the area and \npromote breezy south/southwest winds and highs in the low to \nmid 80s. These seasonably warm temperatures will continue into \nWednesday as the surface trough stalls over the area and washes \nout. This may have a modest effect on temperatures, though \nimpacts should be minimal as the majority of guidance to this \npoint suggests the return of south winds by Thursday following \nthe eastward shift of the trailing surface high. Along with \nlargely northwest flow aloft, this will support continued highs \nin the low to middle 80s to close out the week. Furthermore, \nwith this upper pattern in place, precipitation chances appear \nminimal over the next seven days.",
